{"sounds":{"intro":"This is Galilean Aramaic of the first century, the everyday language of Jesus and his followers. It is written right to left in the square Hebrew letters of that time. The Latin shown is a simple transliteration. No one speaks it as a mother tongue today, so the vowels are reconstructed.","rows":[["a","ah, like \"father\"","abba"],["e","eh, like \"bed\"","ena"],["i","ee, like \"see\"","rabbi"],["o / u","oh and oo","yoma, qum"],["kh","like \"ch\" in Scottish \"loch\" (the letter khet)","lakhma"],["k","a plain \"k\" (the letter kaf)","kokhva"],["q","a \"k\" made further back in the throat","qum"],["'","a throaty catch in the voice (the letter ayin)","ar'a"],["ts","like \"ts\" in \"cats\" (the letter tsade)","tsave"],["sh","like \"sh\" in \"ship\"","shlama"]],"notes":["Stress is usually on the last syllable.","Aramaic has no capital letters and no verb \"to be\" in the present: \"ana gavra\" is \"I a man\", meaning \"I am a man\".","This pool is much smaller than the Spanish one on purpose. No one can record a living speaker of first-century Galilean Aramaic, so only forms that are well attested (in the Gospels' Aramaic words, the Targums and the Talmud) are included. The forms and the script have not been checked by a native reader. Treat it as a first guide."]},"numbers":[{"id":"1-to-10","title":"1 to 10","items":[["one","had","","חד"],["two","tren","","תרין"],["three","tlat","","תלת"],["four","arba","","ארבע"],["five","khamesh","","חמש"],["six","shet","","שת"],["seven","shva","","שבע"],["eight","tmane","","תמני"],["nine","tsha","","תשע"],["ten","asar","","עשר"]]},{"id":"order-and-amounts","title":"Order and amounts","items":[["first","qadmaya","","קדמיא"],["second","tinyana","","תניינא"],["one hundred","me'a","","מאה"],["one thousand","alf","","אלף"],["a little","z'er","","זעיר"],["many","saggi","","סגי"]]}],"greetings":[{"id":"hello-and-goodbye","title":"Hello and goodbye","items":[["peace (hello)","shlama","","שלמא"],["peace be with you","shlam